  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Maintain a comprehensive NHS free at point of need with at least £540m extra annually, restore free eye/dental checks, freeze prescription charges, carers’ benefit, maximum six‑month treatment waits; end rough sleeping by 2000, build more affordable homes, tenant repair rights, new hostels; raise tax thresholds funded by a new 50% rate over £100k, regionally variable minimum wage, childcare strategy, restore benefits for 16–17s; equal pay enforcement, ban discrimination in housing/employment (including armed forces), LGBT equality (equal age of consent, repeal Section 28), civil rights for disabled people, merged equality bodies and statutory employment code, stronger action on racist/homophobic violence; home insulation prioritising low‑income households; Bill of Rights/ECHR incorporation, Human Rights Commission, FOI, stronger regulators; environmental targets (CO2 cuts, pollution taxes), animal welfare. TEXT_G: Calls for a referendum on EU constitutional changes due to transfer of powers; proposes a question contrasting a federal Europe vs an association of sovereign nations; urges timing before/alongside the next election; emphasizes public consent and a fair national debate.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate’s highest priorities are Care and Equality, and TEXT_D is saturated with concrete commitments to reduce harm and improve welfare (NHS expansion, homelessness reduction, affordable housing) and to advance equal treatment and protections (anti‑discrimination, equal pay, LGBT and disability rights, tax/benefit changes and minimum wage). TEXT_G focuses mainly on Authority and Loyalty through sovereignty and constitutional consent, with little direct emphasis on Care or Equality, so it aligns less with the given profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
